WEBVTT

00:00:00.790 --> 00:00:34.000
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. So when I was 36 years old, the year I started This American Life, my relationship with my parents was not the greatest. I'd been working at NPR since I was 19, and they were not into it-- at all. They were not into public radio. They were not into me working in public radio. They saw public radio as this, like, sad little backwater. When they would listen to All Things Considered and Morning Edition, which were shows I was working at, I remember they would complain like, why are the stories so long?
<v Ira Glass>这里是芝加哥 WBEZ 电台，欢迎收听《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。我36岁那年，也就是我创办《美国生活》的那一年，我和父母的关系并不是很好。我从19岁起就在全国公共广播电台（NPR）工作，但他们一点儿也不喜欢——完全不。他们不喜欢公共广播。他们不喜欢我在公共广播工作。在他们看来，公共广播就像是一个可悲的落后小地方。每当他们收听《综合报道》和《早间版》——这些都是我当时参与制作的节目——我记得他们会抱怨说，为什么故事总是这么长？

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:53.440
<v Ira Glass>They especially did not like that I wasn't making much money. I did not make much money. They were both people raised in families where there was never any money. And they really organized their lives to get themselves firmly into the middle class. And they really did not understand why I didn't want to make money.
<v Ira Glass>他们尤其不喜欢我赚不到什么钱。我确实没赚多少钱。他们俩都是在从来没有什么钱的家庭里长大的。他们非常努力地规划生活，好让自己稳固地迈入中产阶级。他们真的无法理解为什么我不想赚钱。

00:00:53.440 --> 00:01:09.110
<v Ira Glass>And then also, there had been a period in my early 20s when I was kind of judgy about certain choices that they were making, and I hurt their feelings. And by my 30s, I tried to make amends and fix that, but it still wasn't quite right between us. And really, I felt pretty distant from them.
<v Ira Glass>而且，在我20岁出头的时候，曾有一段时间我对他们的一些选择指手画脚，伤了他们的心。到了30多岁时，我曾试图弥补并修复那段关系，但我们之间还是有些不对劲。说实话，我感到和他们相当疏远。

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:34.110
<v Ira Glass>They did not hide the fact that they disapproved of pretty much all my life choices, and I didn't feel a lot of patience for that disapproval. And I didn't have a combative relationship with them, but it was just distant. I would go a month or two all the time without talking to them. And they were busy people, but I am sure this hurt their feelings. We talked about it later in our lives.
<v Ira Glass>他们毫不掩饰自己对我的几乎所有生活选择都不满，而我对这种不满也没有多少耐性。我和他们之间倒没有什么针锋相对的冲突，但就是疏远。我经常有一两个月不和他们说话。他们都是忙碌的人，但我确信这伤了他们的心。我们在后来的生活中谈起过这件事。

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:59.780
<v Ira Glass>And then I started the radio show. I have to say, the single most surprising thing that happened in my life because of the radio show is that it fundamentally changed things between me and my parents. It healed things in a way I had not suspected could ever happen. And what I'm going to do today is I'm going to play you four of those conversations that I had on the air with my parents. I'm going to talk about that change.
<v Ira Glass>后来我创办了这个广播节目。我必须说，因为这个广播节目而在我身上发生的、最让人意想不到的事情，就是它从根本上改变了我跟父母之间的关系。它以一种我从未料想过会发生的方式疗愈了我们的关系。我今天要做的，就是播放我和父母在节目中进行过的其中四段对话。我要聊聊这种改变。

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:18.500
<v Ira Glass>A lot of the change happened, I think, because I was just including them in this big project I was doing. I would have them on the show, and they were part of this project that meant so much to me. They were on the show five times in the first year that we were on the air. Really, it wasn't even the first year. It was just, like, the first eight months.
<v Ira Glass>这种改变之所以发生了很多，我想，是因为我把他们纳入了我正在做的这个宏大项目中。我会邀请他们上节目，他们成了这个对我意义重大的项目的一部分。在我们开播的第一年里，他们上过五次节目。真的，甚至都不是第一年。其实也就是前八个月左右。

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:45.860
<v Ira Glass>And we just got into a rhythm of that, and they really liked it. They liked the attention from me. They liked being on the air. They each, but especially my mom, had a kind of performy, hammy side. I'll play you some of these. I think you'll be able to hear that. And I never asked them about this, but I think they also liked what a kind of public sort of affirmation it was of them as parents. Like, oh, see, we're a nice family. Like, see, they were good parents.
<v Ira Glass>我们渐渐适应了这种节奏，他们真的很喜欢。他们喜欢我给予的关注。他们喜欢上节目。他们每个人——尤其是我的妈妈——都有点爱表演、爱出风头的特质。我会放给你们听听看。我想你们能听出来。而且我从未问过他们这件事，但我猜他们也很喜欢这种公开的肯定，这在某种程度上是对他们作为父母的一种认可。就像是，哦，看呐，我们是一个美满的家庭。就像是，看呐，他们是好父母。

00:02:45.860 --> 00:03:14.440
<v Ira Glass>And it really did change things between us. My parents are both dead now, and it still kind of floors me as this lucky thing in my life that I just stumbled into. And so what I'm going to do today is play some of their appearances on the radio show, and maybe jump in, just a little, here and there, to point out things that I am noticing. And there's one that I'm going to play at the end. This is a conversation with my mom that's still-- honestly, it makes my skin crawl today the way it did the day we recorded it.
<v Ira Glass>这确实改变了我们之间的关系。我的父母现在都已经过世了，每当想起这桩我误打误撞得来的生活中的幸运事，我依然会感到由衷的震惊。所以今天我要做的，就是播放他们几次上广播节目的录音，然后也许偶尔在这里和那里插话几句，指出一些我注意到的事情。最后还有一段录音我要放。那是和我妈妈的一段对话，坦白说——直到今天，它依然让我浑身起鸡皮疙瘩，就像我们录音的那天一样。

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:29.380
<v Ira Glass>This whole episode today, the way this came about is that about a year ago, we started doing these bonus episodes that we put out in our podcast feed. And so I'm constantly trying to think of behind-the-scenes stuff to share with listeners in these bonus episodes.
<v Ira Glass>今天整期节目，这个点子的由来是这样的：大约一年前，我们开始在播客动态里发布一些花絮节目。所以我一直在绞尽脑汁地想一些幕后花絮，好在这些花絮节目中跟听众分享。

00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:49.320
<v Ira Glass>And that led me back to relisten to these old interviews with my parents. And then a couple of weeks ago, I put these four interviews into a bonus episode. And honestly, it came out so nicely that we thought we should put this out as an actual regular episode of our show, one that anybody could hear. And so that's what you're hearing right now.
<v Ira Glass>这让我回过头去重新听了这些采访我父母的老录音。然后几周前，我把这四次采访剪辑成了一期花絮节目。说实话，效果出奇的好，以至于我们觉得应该把它作为我们节目的正式常规单集发布出来，让所有人都能听到。这就是你们现在听到的内容。

00:03:49.320 --> 00:04:11.030
<v Ira Glass>And where I want to start things, this first excerpt is from an episode that we did called "Adult Children." This is the fourth time I had one of my parents on the radio show. This is May 1996, which means that my mom, in this recording, is younger than I am right now. She is 60 years old in this recording.
<v Ira Glass>我打算从这里开始，第一个片段出自我们制作过的一期名为《成年子女》的节目。这是我第四次请我的父母之一上广播节目。这是1996年5月，这意味着我妈妈在这个录音里的年纪比现在的我还要年轻。在这段录音里，她60岁。

00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:42.710
<v Ira Glass>And I picked this one to begin because it's one that directly addresses the actual tensions between my parents and their three children, though, as you'll hear, it addresses them in kind of a light way that does not get too deep or heavy. But you can also feel that there's something real under the surface, I think. OK, so I'm going to start this with the opening of that episode, where I tell a little story, very brief story, that kind of sets up the interview that then will happen with my mom. Here we go.
<v Ira Glass>我挑这一段作为开场，是因为它直接触及了我的父母和他们三个成年子女之间实际存在的紧张关系，不过，正如你们将听到的那样，它是以一种轻松的方式来处理的，没有挖得太深或显得过于沉重。但我想，你也能感觉到水面之下有些真实的东西存在。好了，我要用那期节目的开场来切入，我在开场里讲了一个小故事，一个非常简短的故事，它为接下来将要和我妈妈进行的采访做了一个铺垫。我们开始吧。

00:04:42.710 --> 00:05:16.260
<v Ira Glass>Well, when I picked up the phone, it was my mom. And it had been about a month since we had spoken. And as usual, that was my fault. Anyway, she said that she had been invited to speak with a group of women at the local Hadassah, the Jewish Women's Organization. My mom's a therapist in the Jewish suburbs outside Baltimore. And these Hadassah women have this group that meets regularly. All of them are women in their-- somewhere-- I guess--
<v Ira Glass>好吧，当我接起电话时，打来的是我妈妈。我们已经有一个月左右没说过话了。和往常一样，那是我的错。总之，她说她受邀去给当地哈达萨（Hadassah，一个犹太妇女组织）的一群女性做演讲。我妈妈是巴尔的摩郊外犹太人聚居区的心理治疗师。这些哈达萨的女性有一个定期聚会的群体。她们全都是年纪在——某个——我想——

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:28.780
<v Ira Glass>I'm just going to jump in again here in 2026. I am reading this in such a heavy way. [LAUGHS] Hearing it now, I am really trying to milk the drama out of this. Anyway, back to 1996 me.
<v Ira Glass>在这里，时间来到2026年，我打算再次插话。我读这段时的语气太沉重了。[笑声] 现在听来，我真的在极力榨取这里面的戏剧性效果。总之，回到1996年的我。

00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:41.980
<v Ira Glass>All of them are women in their-- somewhere-- I guess in the late 40s to early 60s. And when the group first started meeting, apparently, they discussed all sorts of stuff. It was wide-ranging.
<v Ira Glass>她们全都是年纪在——某个——我想大概是40岁后期到60岁前期之间的女性。当这个群体刚开始聚会时，显然，她们讨论各种各样的事情。涉及面很广。

00:05:41.980 --> 00:06:20.330
<v Ira Glass>But as time progressed, they realized there was only one topic they all really wanted to talk about, only one topic they all needed to talk about, and that was their relationships with their adult children. And at some point, that became the only thing the group discussed. It became its official reason for existence. They had such trauma. And they didn't know what to make of what was going on between them and their adult children. And they invited my mom to lead a discussion on how to get along with your adult children.
<v Ira Glass>但随着时间推移，她们发现只有一个话题是她们所有人都真正想聊的，只有这一个话题是她们所有人都需要聊的，那就是她们与成年子女的关系。在某个时刻，这成了该群体讨论的唯一事情。它成了这个组织存在的官方理由。她们有着那么多的精神创伤。而且她们不知道该如何面对自己与成年子女之间发生的一切。于是她们邀请我妈妈来主导一场讨论，主题是如何与你的成年子女相处。

00:06:20.330 --> 00:06:55.710
<v Ira Glass>So as for preparation-- my mom's a big preparer, and she does research and looks up articles and calls experts. Anyway, as part of this preparation, she decided to call her own three adult children. By the time she called me, my mom had already called my older sister, Randi, and she asked my sister what advice she would give the group. Randi's advice was brief and to the point-- tell them to get a different leader.
<v Ira Glass>至于准备工作——我妈妈非常擅长做准备，她会做研究、查阅文章并打电话给专家。总之，作为准备工作的一部分，她决定打电话给她自己的三个成年子女。在她打给我之前，我妈妈已经打给了我的姐姐兰迪（Randi），她问我姐姐会给这个群体什么建议。兰迪的建议简明扼要——告诉她们换一个领头人。

