<v Ira Glass>All right. I'm just gonna stop the tape. All right, listen, all of you in the audience right now. Let's just agree right now, it's the very beginning of our relationship. It's the very beginning of our radio relationship right now. This is our little first little radio date. And I just don't want any idealizing. No idealizing.
<v Ira Glass>好吧。我来按一下暂停。好吧，听着，现在在座的各位听众。咱们现在就达成共识，这可是我们关系的最初开始。这正是我们广播情缘的最初起点。这是我们的第一次小小的广播约会。我可不希望有任何理想化。绝对不要理想化。

00:19:38.190 --> 00:19:46.630
<v Mom>--is where there's more reality-based relationships. They kind of run away from it because it's not as exciting.
<v Mom>——也就是更趋于现实的恋爱关系时。他们往往会选择逃避，因为这没那么刺激。

00:19:46.630 --> 00:20:13.530
<v Ira Glass>It's interesting that you say that, because actually, as we've approached the first show, I've realized that I am much more comfortable with the notion of everyday, workaday sort of radio work, and being on every week, and having pieces on the air. But the notion of saying, in a really big way, "OK, this is the beginning. It's the beginning, and we're going to have a big beginning, or we're going to make an epic statement," I feel very uncomfortable with.
<v Ira Glass>你这么说挺有意思的，因为事实上，随着第一期节目的临近，我发现自己对于日常的、例行的广播工作，以及每周都做节目、有稿子播出的这种状态，要适应得多。但是，如果用一种非常宏大的方式去说：“好吧，这是一个新的开始。这是一个开始，我们要有一个盛大的开端，或者我们要发表一番划时代的宣言，”对此我感到非常不自在。

00:20:13.530 --> 00:20:16.050
<v Mom>Hmm. So you are good at middles.
<v Mom>唔。所以你比较擅长过程。

00:20:16.050 --> 00:20:18.410
<v Ira Glass>I'm better, I think, at middles than at beginnings.
