<v Johnnie Ray>(SINGING) Just walkin' in the rain
<v Johnnie Ray>（唱歌）雨中漫步

00:31:28.960 --> 00:31:53.800
<v Ira Glass>Now, the way a story like this usually ends is that I reveal that this is why I'm in radio-- to make good on my father's legacy, to live the dream that he never got to live. But I have to say, the fact is, I never knew him as a radio DJ. I did not know that he had ever done radio the entire time I grew up. It was never discussed.
<v Ira Glass>通常情况下，像这样的故事结局是这样的：我揭晓我之所以从事广播行业，是为了继承我父亲的遗志，去实现他从未能实现的梦想。但我必须得说，实际情况是，我从来不知道他曾当过广播DJ。在我成长的整个过程中，我根本不知道他做过广播。我们从没讨论过这件事。

00:31:53.800 --> 00:32:22.390
<v Ira Glass>The recordings of him doing this stuff were packed away in the basement. Nobody talked about it. I never thought of him as somebody who did anything but certified public accounting. If my mom ever mentioned this, I do not remember it. And I certainly didn't grow up with any special feeling about radio. I could care less about radio. Like most Americans, my medium of choice was television.
<v Ira Glass>他做这些事情的录音被打包收在地下室里。从来没有人谈起过。我从没想过他除了做注册会计师之外还做过别的什么。如果我妈妈曾经提起过，我也毫无印象。而且我在成长过程中，对广播也绝对没有任何特殊的情结。我对广播可以说是漠不关心。和大多数美国人一样，我首选的媒介是电视。

00:32:22.390 --> 00:32:48.310
<v Ira Glass>I got my first job with National Public Radio in Washington when I was 19 years old. And at that point, I simply wanted a job in the media. If an ad agency-- I was, like, a freshman in college. If an ad agency had hired me that summer, right now, I would probably be doing Bud Light commercials, you know? I could just see the whole thing laying out that way.
<v Ira Glass>我在19岁时得到了在华盛顿美国国家公共广播电台（NPR）的第一份工作。当时，我只是单纯地想要一份媒体方面的工作。如果当时有一家广告公司——我那时大概是个大学一年级新生。如果那年夏天有一家广告公司雇用了我，现在的我可能就在拍百威淡啤的商业广告了，你知道吗？我甚至能预见到整条人生轨迹就是那样铺开的。

00:32:48.310 --> 00:33:26.020
<v Ira Glass>And in 1978, when I started working at NPR, my parents absolutely did not want me to do it. It wasn't even, like, a judgment call. They were completely against it for the next, I want to say, 10 years. Maybe it was 15 years, really. I mean, they simply saw it like the way most parents would. They saw it as impractical. They worried that I would never make any decent money, never be able to support a family-- essentially, the reasons that my father quit radio.
<v Ira Glass>1978年我开始在NPR工作时，我的父母坚决反对我这么做。这甚至都不是那种需要斟酌的事情。在接下来的，我想说整整10年里，他们是完全反对的。也许其实是15年。我的意思是，他们的看法和绝大多数父母一样。他们觉得这很不切实际。他们担心我永远也赚不到体面的收入，永远无法养家糊口——本质上，这也是我父亲当年辞去广播工作的原因。

00:33:26.020 --> 00:34:00.580
<v Ira Glass>When I first dug out these tapes of my father about a year ago, I asked my dad if he ever wished he could still do radio, and he was completely unenthusiastic about the idea. In all my life, I had only seen his desire to be on the radio once. And this was actually a couple years ago. I was filling in as the guest host of Talk of the Nation, this daily call-in show NPR does out of Washington. I was doing that for half a year. And my father had never seen me host a radio show.
<v Ira Glass>大约在一年前，当我第一次翻出这些我父亲的磁带时，我问过他是否还曾希望能够继续做广播，而他对这个想法完全表现得毫无热情。在我的一生中，我只见过他渴望上广播节目一次。那其实是几年前的事。当时我作为客座主持，代班主持NPR在华盛顿制作的每日电话连线节目《国事论坛》（Talk of the Nation）。这份工作我做了半年。而我父亲从未看过我主持广播节目。

00:34:00.580 --> 00:34:19.210
<v Ira Glass>And he and my mom drove down to Washington from Baltimore to watch me do the show. And before the show, he asked me if he could read the news. And at first, I thought he was joking. And I don't remember exactly what I said back. I joked something back at him.
<v Ira Glass>他和妈妈从巴尔的摩开车来到华盛顿看我主持节目。在节目开始前，他问我他能不能宣读新闻。起初，我以为他在开玩笑。我不记得当时具体回了他什么。我开玩笑地应付了他一句。

00:34:19.210 --> 00:34:43.730
<v Ira Glass>And then later, as we got closer to going on the air, he asked again, that he would like to read the newscast, the NPR newscast at the top of the hour at the beginning of the show. And I realized he was serious, and I had to explain to him that I didn't have any authority over that, that the NPR newscasters, they wrote their own news, and they delivered it themselves.
<v Ira Glass>后来，随着上节目时间的临近，他又问了一次，说他想播报新闻，也就是节目刚开始时的整点NPR新闻简报。我意识到他是认真的，我不得不向他解释我对此没有决定权，NPR的新闻播报员都是自己撰写新闻并亲自播报的。

00:34:43.730 --> 00:35:15.770
<v Ira Glass>Then a few months after that, he and I were talking about what he's going to do after he retires. He still works, still works long, long hours. And he told me he's been thinking about doing a little bit of radio work again after he retires. That is, if he can find some radio station or some radio program that could use him in some way.
<v Ira Glass>在那之后的几个月里，我和他讨论过他退休后要做什么。他还在工作，依然在工作极长的时间。他告诉我，他一直在考虑等退休后再做一点广播工作。也就是看看能不能找到某个广播电台或某档广播节目，能在某种程度上用得上他。

00:35:15.770 --> 00:35:33.190
<v Ira Glass>OK, it's me again in 2026. So not that long after that, just four months after that, I asked my dad to co-host the show with me for Father's Day, so he did get his chance to get back on the radio. This is an episode that we do rerun now and then. So I'm just going to play you a little snippet or two from this.
<v Ira Glass>好了，2026年的我又回来了。所以在那之后不久，仅仅四个月后，我邀请我爸爸在父亲节和我共同主持这档节目，所以他确实得到了重返广播的机会。这是一集我们会不时重播的节目。所以我打算放一两个小片段给大家听听。

00:35:33.190 --> 00:35:34.930
<v Ira Glass>OK, so Dad, so you have the script.
<v Ira Glass>好了，爸爸，剧本在你手上了。

00:35:34.930 --> 00:35:42.470
<v Dad>I have the script. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life, the Father's Day edition.
<v Dad>剧本在我手上。来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这里是《此生美国》（This American Life）父亲节特辑。

00:35:42.470 --> 00:35:44.550
<v Ira Glass>Dad, you are such a pro.
<v Ira Glass>爸爸，你太专业了。

00:35:44.550 --> 00:35:52.510
<v Dad>[LAUGHS]
<v Dad>（笑）

00:35:52.510 --> 00:35:56.370
<v Dad>I haven't done this in 40 years. It brings back all kinds of memories.
<v Dad>我已经40年没干过这行了。这勾起了我各种各样的回忆。

00:35:56.370 --> 00:36:01.090
<v Ira Glass>Now, you better explain to our radio listeners in what context you actually sat in front of a radio microphone, Dad.
<v Ira Glass>爸爸，你最好向我们的广播听众解释一下，你当年是在什么情况下坐在广播麦克风前的。

00:36:01.090 --> 00:36:02.870
<v Dad>Well--
<v Dad>嗯——

00:36:02.870 --> 00:36:08.470
<v Ira Glass>And then my dad kind of recaps what you already heard about his radio career. I'm just going to pick up now a little later.
<v Ira Glass>然后我爸爸大致回顾了你们刚才听到的他的广播生涯。我直接跳到稍微靠后一点的地方。

00:36:08.470 --> 00:36:14.210
<v Ira Glass>And today for Father's Day, my co-host will be my own father, Barry Glass, certified public accountant.
<v Ira Glass>今天为了庆祝父亲节，我的联合主持人将是我自己的父亲，巴里·格拉斯（Barry Glass），注册会计师。

00:36:14.210 --> 00:36:15.650
<v Dad>And it's a real kick to do this.
<v Dad>能做这个节目真的很有意思。

00:36:15.650 --> 00:36:18.040
<v Ira Glass>I know. This is our little Father's Day adventure together.
<v Ira Glass>我知道。这是我们俩一起经历的小小父亲节冒险。

00:36:18.040 --> 00:36:30.780
<v Dad>You could have bought me a tie.
<v Dad>你本可以给我买条领带的。

00:36:30.780 --> 00:36:32.940
<v Ira Glass>Dad, why don't you read the billboard?
<v Ira Glass>爸爸，你为什么不读一下节目预告呢？

00:36:32.940 --> 00:36:55.060
<v Dad>Our program today will have four acts. Act One, "Sandra Tsing Loh Finds Out That the World Sees Her Father Very Differently from the Way She Sees Him." Act Two, "Dad's Music." We have a story from writer Sherman Alexie. Act Three, "The Moment Dad Left." Act Four, "Reconciling with Dad," a story from playwright Beau O'Reilly.
<v Dad>我们今天的节目有四个部分。第一部分，“桑德拉·清·罗（Sandra Tsing Loh）发现，世人眼中的父亲与她眼中的父亲大不相同”。第二部分，“爸爸的音乐”。我们有一篇来自作家谢尔曼·亚历西（Sherman Alexie）的故事。第三部分，“爸爸离开的那一刻”。第四部分，“与爸爸和解”，这是一个来自剧作家博·奥赖利（Beau O'Reilly）的故事。

00:36:55.060 --> 00:37:15.110
<v Ira Glass>I just want to say, we did this at the beginning of all the episodes in that first year or two. We would list each act and say what the act would be. And then at some point-- I don't know how many years in it was-- I realized like, oh, we don't have to do that. Like, if we do the beginning of the show right, people would just want to hear what's going to happen next. And they kind of don't even care what's coming up.
<v Ira Glass>我只想说，在第一年或头两年的所有节目开头，我们都会这样做。我们会列出每一部分，并说明每个部分的内容是什么。后来在某个时间点——我不记得是做了多少年之后——我突然意识到，哦，我们其实没必要这么做。如果我们的节目开头做得好，人们自然会想听接下来会发生什么。而且他们其实根本不在乎接下来要播什么。

00:37:15.110 --> 00:37:30.890
<v Ira Glass>Like, all you just want to know is, I guess it's going to be good today. And then listing the acts like he just did, it just feels like it doesn't mean that much. You know what I mean? Unless you're a fan of one of those people in particular. But otherwise, no, we don't do that very much anymore.
<v Ira Glass>就像你只想知道，今天的节目应该会很精彩吧。然后像他刚才那样逐一列出各个部分，就让人觉得没那么大意义了。你知道我的意思吗？除非你是其中某位特定嘉宾的粉丝。但除此之外，不，我们现在不怎么那样做了。

00:37:30.890 --> 00:37:59.610
<v Ira Glass>I will say, my dad really, really liked being on the air. His friends heard him. That was a really nice thing. And it was just, like, acknowledging this part of his life that, honestly, I didn't know about when I was growing up. There's a thing that happened with me and my dad, and it happened on tape. And for the life of me, I have no idea where this recording is, and I wish I could find it.
<v Ira Glass>我得承认，我爸爸非常非常喜欢上节目。他的朋友们都听到了。那真是一件很棒的事。这就好像是对他人生中这一部分的认可，老实说，我在成长过程中对此一无所知。我和我爸爸之间曾发生过一件事，而且它是被录在磁带里的。天地良心，我完全不知道这段录音现在在哪里，我多么希望自己能找到它。

00:37:59.610 --> 00:38:25.740
<v Ira Glass>And that is, when we were taping for some episode, we got into a very real conversation, and the tape just kept rolling. And I don't remember how we got on it, but it was just-- it really was about, was he a good dad? And he was really asking in a real way. And then I felt like, I will try to meet him in this moment. And I had been in therapy for a while.
<v Ira Glass>那就是，当我们为某集节目录音时，我们进行了一次非常坦诚的对话，而录音机一直在运转。我不记得我们是怎么聊到这个话题的，但那真的——那真的是在探讨，他是一个好爸爸吗？他是真的很认真地在问。然后我觉得，我会在这一刻试着去回应他。我当时已经做了一段时间的心理咨询。

00:38:25.740 --> 00:39:02.160
<v Ira Glass>And my dad, I will say, was a very well-intentioned dad who really did try to do his best. But his dad left when he was four or five years old. He didn't grow up with a dad. And he just didn't know what it would be to be a dad. He really was inventing it as he went along. And he could be very-- he would just get angry at random stuff in ways that I really felt like I spent my childhood kind of dodging around and trying to anticipate and trying to read his moods.
<v Ira Glass>我得说，我爸爸是一个很有善意的爸爸，他也确实尽力做到了最好。但他四五岁时他父亲就离开了。他成长过程中没有父亲。他根本不知道当一个父亲是怎么回事。他真的是在边走边摸索。而且他可能会非常——他会莫名其妙地对一些小事生气，以至于我真的觉得自己整个童年都在小心翼翼地闪躲、揣摩，试图看他的脸色行事。

00:39:02.160 --> 00:39:39.790
<v Ira Glass>And basically, I said there were just-- I explained to him there were ways that he treated us that really affected the way that I treat everyone in the world today, and went into maybe a little more depth than I'm doing with you here right now, because you are not my father. And I said it really-- it was hard. And I said, I've been in therapy. I'm trying to change the way I am with other people, so I'm not so alert all the time to how they're going to treat me, and just trust people a little more. And I just kind of laid out things in a way.
<v Ira Glass>基本上，我当时说的是——我向他解释，他对待我们的某些方式，确实深刻影响了我今天对待世界上所有人的方式。而且我讲得可能比我现在对你讲的要稍微深入一些，因为你不是我父亲。我说这真的——真的很不容易。我说，我一直在做心理咨询。我正试图改变我与他人相处的方式，这样我就不会总是那么高度警惕别人会怎么对待我，而是去稍微多信任别人一点。我就是把这些事情剖析出来。

00:39:39.790 --> 00:40:08.550
<v Ira Glass>And he heard this, and he paused. And he said-- honestly, he said, like, the perfect thing. He paused, and he said, I'm so sorry. That must have been so tough for you. And that must be so tough for you. And he said, look, I was doing my best. I didn't know. I didn't have a dad. I didn't know how to do it. I really was trying.
<v Ira Glass>他听了这些，停顿了一下。然后他说——老实说，他说的话简直完美。他停顿了一下，说道：“我很抱歉。你当时一定吃了很多苦。你当时一定经历了很难熬的日子。”接着他说：“听着，我当时在尽我所能。我什么都不知道。我没有爸爸，我不知道该怎么当父亲。我真的是在努力。”

00:40:08.550 --> 00:40:44.730
<v Ira Glass>And then we never talked about it again. And we really-- honestly, all the drama between me and my dad for my whole childhood, I know this sounds like a complete exaggeration to say, this is true, but I swear, emotionally, for me anyway, it really felt like in one conversation that was maybe eight minutes long, it was completely resolved, because he did the simple thing of saying that he heard me and just said he was sorry and he was trying, which I believe completely.
<v Ira Glass>然后我们再也没有谈起过这件事。老实说，我和我爸爸整个童年之间的所有冲突——我知道听起来可能完全是夸大其词，但这千真万确，我发誓，从情感层面上对我来说，真的就像是在大约八分钟的一场对话中，所有问题都彻底解决了，因为他做了一件很简单的事，那就是说他听到了我的心声，并说他很抱歉，而且他当时一直在努力——对此我深信不疑。

00:40:44.730 --> 00:41:12.280
<v Ira Glass>And very much in contrast-- and again, my mom is dead, so it feels a little weird to just say things about her here on this show. But I don't know. Why talk about this if I'm not going to be real? My mom was much more defensive, even though she was a therapist. If my sisters and I would try to talk to her about, you do this thing, and you kind of-- whatever it was, she just could not hear it. She really couldn't.
<v Ira Glass>这形成了鲜明的对比——再说一次，我妈妈已经去世了，所以在这个节目里谈论她让我觉得有点奇怪。但管他呢。如果不打算说真话，还谈论这些干嘛？我妈妈的防御心要强得多，尽管她是个心理咨询师。如果我和姐姐们试图跟她说，你总是这样，你有点——无论是什么事，她就是听不进去。她真的听不进去。

