<v Mom>[笑] 我们总是以这种方式结束对话，对吧？

00:52:47.580 --> 00:52:53.180
<v Ira Glass>With that particular advice. Yeah, that's-- I know. [LAUGHS] I know. I could ask you any question--
<v Ira Glass>带着那个特定的建议。没错，那是——我知道。[笑] 我知道。我可以问你任何问题——

00:52:53.180 --> 00:52:53.680
<v Mom>That's right.
<v Mom>没错。

00:52:53.380 --> 00:52:54.640
<v Ira Glass>--and that would be the advice.
<v Ira Glass>——而得到的都会是这个建议。

00:52:54.640 --> 00:52:57.410
<v Mom>Well, that was the first rule of journalism you taught me.
<v Mom>好吧，这可是你教给我的第一条新闻原则。

00:52:57.410 --> 00:52:58.210
<v Ira Glass>Is what?
<v Ira Glass>是什么？

00:52:58.210 --> 00:53:01.470
<v Mom>No matter what they ask you, be sure to get your point in.
<v Mom>不管别人问你什么，务必要把你的观点塞进去。

00:53:01.470 --> 00:53:06.490
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHS] When you were first being interviewed by people, this is what I told you to say.
<v Ira Glass>[笑] 当你刚开始被人采访的时候，这就是我教你要说的话。

00:53:06.490 --> 00:53:07.450
<v Mom>Right.
<v Mom>对。

00:53:07.450 --> 00:53:13.870
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHS] Well, I'm glad-- well, I'm glad we got to that, then.
<v Ira Glass>[笑] 好吧，我很高兴——好吧，很高兴我们聊到了这一点。

00:53:13.870 --> 00:53:18.770
<v Mom>[CHUCKLES]
<v Mom>[轻笑]

00:53:18.770 --> 00:53:26.850
<v Ira Glass>My mom, Dr. Shirley Glass, in Baltimore.
<v Ira Glass>我妈妈，希莉·格拉斯博士，在巴尔的摩。

00:53:26.850 --> 00:53:58.640
<v Ira Glass>I'd just say, hear how nicely we're getting along? We actually-- that's a really sweet conversation to have with your mom. And before these appearances on the radio, on the radio show, I just don't think that would have happened. There's just something so-- we were so in a nice, friendly groove. Even though we are kind of making little points with each other, it's just like a very lovely thing. And it continued that way, more or less, until she died.
<v Ira Glass>我只想说，听听我们相处得有多融洽？我们实际上——跟自己的妈妈进行这样一次谈话真的很温馨。而在这些广播节目、在广播秀露面之前，我觉得这种事是不会发生的。这里面有一种感觉——我们当时处于一种非常愉快、友好的状态。尽管我们俩在互相挑点小刺，但这依然是一件非常美好的事。而且这种状态差不多一直持续到她去世。

00:53:58.640 --> 00:54:22.360
<v Ira Glass>And when we would see each other in real life, not just on the radio, we were more open with each other. It was just easier. They stopped telling me I was wasting my life in radio. And I think, if I imagine it from their point of view, I think that after we did these things on the radio, they knew, when we see each other in real life, that I wasn't going to sit them down and question their choices, question them.
<v Ira Glass>当我们现实生活中见面时，不仅仅是在广播里，我们对彼此更加坦诚了。一切都变得更容易了。他们不再对我说我在广播里是在浪费生命。我想，如果我从他们的角度来想象，我觉得在我们做完这些广播节目之后，他们在现实生活中见面时就会知道，我不会把他们拉坐下来质问他们的选择、质问他们。

00:54:22.360 --> 00:55:02.550
<v Ira Glass>Doing these stories on the radio, it's like we practiced getting along together nicely in public. I think that public act helped them get it into their heads that I really did accept them, and that the period of my life where I questioned them so much in my 20s was really long over. So that's how the radio show changed my life, or definitely one of the biggest ways.
<v Ira Glass>在广播里做这些故事，就像我们在公开场合练习如何友好地相处一样。我想，这种公众行为帮助他们在脑海里确信，我是真的接纳了他们，而我在20年代曾经那样疯狂质问他们的那段人生时期确实已经彻底过去了。所以这就是广播节目改变我人生轨迹的方式，或者绝对是其中最主要的方式之一。

